endobj
637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (In addition, Chester also reflected a structural misalignment between our strategic execution and asset allocation. Specifically, we made mistakes in our initial financial forecasting, including a $23.02M inventory-in-hand in round 2 and underinvestment in automation, which further reduced our ability to achieve economies of scale in our traditional and low-end markets \(Cake & Cedar\). )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
638 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (6.2 What the Team Has Actually Learned)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (After six rounds of simulation, we learned the importance of maintaining sustainable competitive advantages to achieve optimal strategic performance throughout the operational process. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (6.2.1 Forecasting matters more than aggressive production)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (More specifically, the first critical lesson we learned is that forecasting is a significant financial constraint rather than merely an operational target. The results showed poor forecasting capability by the Chester team; we had $23.03M in inventory on hand in round 2, which forced us to take on an enormous current debt, directly triggering a $1.67M emergency loan in round 3. In other words, we created negative path dependency in the early stage, significantly reducing our strategic flexibility due to early debt burdens and restricting us from spending more on R&D and Marketing to respond to competitors' moves in later rounds. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
642 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (GROUP LION)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
